      "message": "In the current backup/restore PR[1] workflow, all related API units pause first, then the related `mysql-router` applications scale to zero before the MySQL restore begins. Scaling the routers down closes the database paths and prevents further writes independently of Traefik’s backend convergence. The ~30s Traefik delay can cause transient HTTP failures during pause/resume, but it should not affect restore consistency. Faster health-check work would improve availability/convergence and seems useful as a follow-up, but I don\u0027t think it needs to block the pause/resume primitive.\n\n[1] https://github.com/canonical/snap-openstack/pull/876\n\nOne possible solution is to reduce the health check timeout, but 30s already seems reasonable. Another approach could be to add a field in Traefik\u0027s databag indicating that the application entered `maintenance`, or some state that signals Traefik to stop routing right away when that field is posted to the app databag. But that would require much more work and coordination than a simple timeout decrease.",
